      "event_type": "command",
      "command": "info",
      "response": "\n\nWelcome to Baltimore:24!\n\nThis game is my first effort at interactive fiction. It is not meant to be a \ngame in the strict sense of the word but rather just a practice exercise for me \nso I could learn the basics of Inform. I am releasing it as freeware so that my \nname could circulate a bit as an interactive fiction author and maybe, just \nmaybe, provide the tiniest bit of entertainment for any poor soul who may find \nthemselves playing.\n\nBaltimore:24 is the true story of one of my many travails as an Internet \nService Provider. I am a Sysop (System Operator) of two public systems, \nExcaliber.Net and Excelsior.Net. I thought of my Easter Sunday disaster with a \nbad router as a a great model to build a work of interactive fiction around. \nThis game is not very elaborate and it occurs to me that I could have been much \nmore detailed in some areas but then the whole point of the exercise becomes \nmoot.\n\nHOWEVER - Now that I am pretty comfortable with Inform I have begun on my first \nserious work of interactive fiction; Quest. Quest is going to be an adventure \ngame in the grand style. Lots of rooms, tons of treasures, quite a few nice \npuzzles and great prose too!\n\nSpecial thanks to Graham Nelson who is the creator of Inform who makes this and \nall of my future works possible.\n\nIn addition, many thanks to Neil James Brown (neil.at.highmount.demon.co.uk) \nfor his constructive criticism, the bugs he brought to my attention and his \ntime altogether for sharing his opinions with me. This is Release 2 of \nBaltimore:24 and it only happened because of him.\n\nVisit my website at http://www.excelsior.net/random and stay tuned. I'll be \nupdating my page quite often with status reports.\n\n\n>",
